1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,920
Imagine you are a dictator. You control
the government. You control the army.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,990
You control the police.
You control the newspapers.

3
00:00:10,145 --> 00:00:13,635
And one morning somebody walks in
with an interesting suggestion.

4
00:00:14,545 --> 00:00:16,095
Why don't we hold free elections?

5
00:00:17,048 --> 00:00:19,018
So you ask the obvious question.

6
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:21,730
And what happens if I lose?

7
00:00:23,259 --> 00:00:25,349
That is the whole problem in one line.

8
00:00:26,279 --> 00:00:29,679
If losing an election costs you
your position, your money, your

9
00:00:29,759 --> 00:00:33,449
freedom, possibly your life,
why would you ever agree to one?

10
00:00:34,130 --> 00:00:37,300
And yet it has happened. More than once.

11
00:00:38,030 --> 00:00:40,450
One of the strangest
examples is an island you

12
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:42,740
have heard of for completely
different reasons.

13
00:00:43,638 --> 00:00:44,838
Taiwan.

14
00:00:46,943 --> 00:00:52,603
In 1949, the Chinese Communist Party won
the civil war on the mainland, and the

15
00:00:52,683 --> 00:00:57,393
government of the Republic of China, led
by the Kuomintang, retreated to Taiwan.

16
00:00:58,367 --> 00:01:02,037
If you know the usual story, you
probably picture what came next.

17
00:01:02,907 --> 00:01:06,537
A small democratic island, safely
out of reach of the mainland.

18
00:01:07,468 --> 00:01:08,668
Not quite.

19
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:11,950
Taiwan was an authoritarian state.

20
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:17,930
Martial law was declared in 1949,
and it stayed in place for 38 years.

21
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:22,830
Opposition was heavily restricted.
The press was controlled.

22
00:01:23,837 --> 00:01:26,987
People suspected of political
opposition could be imprisoned.

23
00:01:28,237 --> 00:01:30,677
In the years known as
the White Terror, tens of

24
00:01:30,757 --> 00:01:34,007
thousands were imprisoned,
and thousands were executed.

25
00:01:34,964 --> 00:01:40,014
This was not a democracy waiting patiently
for its turn. It was a dictatorship.

26
00:01:43,054 --> 00:01:44,624
And then it started to change.

27
00:01:45,454 --> 00:01:46,974
Taiwan industrialised.

28
00:01:47,454 --> 00:01:53,344
Factories, exports, wages. People got
richer. Education expanded. Cities grew.

29
00:01:54,119 --> 00:01:58,029
A large middle class appeared,
which had not really existed before.

30
00:01:58,999 --> 00:02:02,049
And alongside the money, something
less visible was growing.

31
00:02:02,921 --> 00:02:03,961
People formed associations.

32
00:02:04,041 --> 00:02:06,771
Students protested.

33
00:02:07,581 --> 00:02:10,941
Journalists pushed at the edges
of what the censors would allow.

34
00:02:11,021 --> 00:02:15,691
Opposition politicians organised,
even though parties were banned.

35
00:02:16,527 --> 00:02:19,937
The government could
still arrest them. It did.

36
00:02:20,707 --> 00:02:23,247
But a society is easy
to control only while it

37
00:02:23,327 --> 00:02:26,217
stays simple, and this one
was getting complicated.

38
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:31,210
And then, in September
1986, the opposition did

39
00:02:31,290 --> 00:02:33,660
something that was
straightforwardly illegal.

40
00:02:34,670 --> 00:02:36,780
They announced a new political party.

41
00:02:37,611 --> 00:02:40,531
Under martial law, that was
enough to be arrested for.

42
00:02:40,611 --> 00:02:44,701
The government knew about the
meeting. It had the names.

43
00:02:45,668 --> 00:02:46,868
And it did nothing.

44
00:02:49,788 --> 00:02:52,638
The following year,
Taiwan lifted martial law.

45
00:02:53,268 --> 00:02:57,238
The rules on parties and newspapers
came off gradually after that.

46
00:02:57,971 --> 00:03:02,881
In 1996, Taiwan held its first
direct presidential election.

47
00:03:03,031 --> 00:03:06,221
And then came the moment
that actually matters.

48
00:03:07,093 --> 00:03:08,563
The year 2000.

49
00:03:10,179 --> 00:03:14,469
The Kuomintang had run Taiwan
for half a century. And it lost.

50
00:03:15,279 --> 00:03:16,989
The opposition won the presidency.

51
00:03:18,023 --> 00:03:19,613
And the tanks did not roll.

52
00:03:22,553 --> 00:03:24,683
The army did not overturn the count.

53
00:03:25,413 --> 00:03:28,723
Nobody announced that the
result had been misinterpreted.

54
00:03:29,273 --> 00:03:31,383
The government simply changed hands.

55
00:03:32,274 --> 00:03:34,624
The old ruling party carried on existing.

56
00:03:35,534 --> 00:03:36,944
It became the opposition.

57
00:03:38,014 --> 00:03:41,024
And eight years later it won
an election and came back.

58
00:03:41,846 --> 00:03:43,396
Sit with that for a moment.

59
00:03:44,246 --> 00:03:46,846
A party that had run an
authoritarian state for decades

60
00:03:46,926 --> 00:03:50,056
discovered that it could
lose power and survive it.

61
00:03:53,156 --> 00:03:54,926
So why did Taiwan do it?

62
00:03:55,636 --> 00:04:00,306
Did the rulers wake up one morning and
become nice people? That seems unlikely.

63
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:06,630
Does getting richer automatically make
a country democratic? Hold that thought.

64
00:04:07,420 --> 00:04:11,090
Because Taiwan was not the only place
where something like this happened.

65
00:04:11,873 --> 00:04:16,883
Look at South Korea. For decades it
was run by authoritarian governments.

66
00:04:17,573 --> 00:04:20,503
And for decades it was
also doing the other thing.

67
00:04:21,275 --> 00:04:24,615
Industry, exports, schools,
a huge urban middle class.

68
00:04:24,695 --> 00:04:28,945
And by 1987, the protests were enormous.

69
00:04:29,778 --> 00:04:31,728
And there was another
pressure in the room.

70
00:04:32,378 --> 00:04:35,368
Seoul was hosting the Olympic
Games the following year.

71
00:04:35,978 --> 00:04:37,288
The world would be watching.

72
00:04:38,091 --> 00:04:39,831
The protests did not stop.

73
00:04:39,911 --> 00:04:43,441
And the ruling establishment
accepted major reforms.

74
00:04:44,611 --> 00:04:48,601
The June 29 Declaration promised
direct presidential elections.

75
00:04:49,583 --> 00:04:51,253
Notice the shape of that.

76
00:04:51,923 --> 00:04:54,673
It is not simply a
dictator being overthrown.

77
00:04:55,399 --> 00:04:58,319
It is partly the people in power
deciding that keeping the old

78
00:04:58,399 --> 00:05:02,629
system is becoming harder, and
more dangerous, than changing it.

79
00:05:05,079 --> 00:05:06,909
Chile is stranger still.

80
00:05:07,699 --> 00:05:10,099
Augusto Pinochet's military
government had written

81
00:05:10,179 --> 00:05:13,149
a constitution with a
referendum built into it.

82
00:05:13,795 --> 00:05:19,005
In 1988, the country was asked whether
Pinochet should continue as president.

83
00:05:20,135 --> 00:05:22,945
The regime expected the vote
to hand it some legitimacy.

84
00:05:24,048 --> 00:05:25,248
The No side won.

85
00:05:26,299 --> 00:05:27,809
And the result was accepted.

86
00:05:29,199 --> 00:05:33,929
Elections followed in 1989, and
Pinochet left the presidency in 1990.

87
00:05:34,889 --> 00:05:37,329
It was not clean, and it was not simple.

88
00:05:38,068 --> 00:05:42,068
Pinochet remained commander-in-chief
of the army for another eight years.

89
00:05:42,148 --> 00:05:47,238
And the political system still contained
important remnants of the old regime.

90
00:05:48,193 --> 00:05:49,883
But the central fact stands.

91
00:05:50,773 --> 00:05:53,573
A dictator had put his
continuation in office to a vote.

92
00:05:53,653 --> 00:05:55,203
And lost.

93
00:05:57,693 --> 00:06:00,863
Spain did yet another
version of the same thing.

94
00:06:01,113 --> 00:06:06,903
Francisco Franco died in 1975, and
Spain did not collapse into revolution.

95
00:06:07,924 --> 00:06:11,874
Instead, reformers inside the system
began taking the system apart.

96
00:06:12,644 --> 00:06:14,514
Parties were legalised.

97
00:06:15,124 --> 00:06:17,184
Elections were held in 1977.

98
00:06:17,264 --> 00:06:20,974
A new constitution followed in 1978.

99
00:06:21,976 --> 00:06:27,686
Not a revolution. Not the destruction of
the old political class. A transition.

100
00:06:28,711 --> 00:06:30,311
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

101
00:06:30,391 --> 00:06:33,121
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

102
00:06:33,971 --> 00:06:35,461
It really helps the channel.

103
00:06:38,231 --> 00:06:42,941
So there is a pattern here, and it is
not that dictators become democrats.

104
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:46,160
It is that a regime can
reach a point where its own

105
00:06:46,240 --> 00:06:49,250
leaders start asking a
completely different question.

106
00:06:49,820 --> 00:06:51,870
Not how do we hold on forever.

107
00:06:52,739 --> 00:06:55,809
But how do we change this
without destroying ourselves.

108
00:06:56,379 --> 00:06:59,729
Which brings us to the thing
this whole video is really about.

109
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:01,760
The exit problem.

110
00:07:04,770 --> 00:07:06,580
Imagine two dictatorships.

111
00:07:07,090 --> 00:07:11,280
In the first one, the ruling party
knows roughly what losing looks like.

112
00:07:11,993 --> 00:07:15,793
We would still be allowed to
exist. We would keep our houses.

113
00:07:15,873 --> 00:07:19,843
We would keep our businesses. We
could stand in the next election.

114
00:07:19,973 --> 00:07:21,263
We might even win it.

115
00:07:21,992 --> 00:07:24,602
Losing is unpleasant. It is not the end.

116
00:07:25,412 --> 00:07:26,842
Now the second dictatorship.

117
00:07:27,957 --> 00:07:29,607
If we lose, they take everything.

118
00:07:30,517 --> 00:07:31,907
Our families are prosecuted.

119
00:07:32,757 --> 00:07:34,947
Our allies go to prison. So do we.

120
00:07:35,630 --> 00:07:36,830
Possibly worse.

121
00:07:37,753 --> 00:07:41,943
Now ask which of those two groups is
more likely to agree to a free election.

122
00:07:42,913 --> 00:07:44,403
It is not a hard question.

123
00:07:45,232 --> 00:07:48,962
The second group has every reason
to hold on, whatever that costs.

124
00:07:49,232 --> 00:07:52,642
Even if the country would
be better off without them.

125
00:07:53,309 --> 00:07:56,419
Even if they would privately
prefer a quiet way out.

126
00:07:57,209 --> 00:08:00,219
Their survival is now welded
to the survival of the regime.

127
00:08:01,193 --> 00:08:03,803
And at that point, democracy
stops being a reform.

128
00:08:04,673 --> 00:08:07,563
It becomes a gamble with
their own lives on the table.

129
00:08:10,683 --> 00:08:14,613
Which may explain one of the strangest
comparisons in modern politics.

130
00:08:15,463 --> 00:08:18,753
After Mao Zedong died,
China changed enormously.

131
00:08:19,699 --> 00:08:23,109
Deng Xiaoping and the leaders
after him opened up the economy.

132
00:08:23,939 --> 00:08:25,289
Markets expanded.

133
00:08:26,119 --> 00:08:28,329
The country became dramatically richer.

134
00:08:29,121 --> 00:08:30,731
Education expanded.

135
00:08:31,501 --> 00:08:33,491
An enormous middle class appeared.

136
00:08:34,281 --> 00:08:37,071
China became deeply connected
to the world economy.

137
00:08:37,968 --> 00:08:41,308
In other words, China went through
a transformation that, in other

138
00:08:41,388 --> 00:08:44,878
countries, had sometimes gone
alongside political liberalisation.

139
00:08:46,789 --> 00:08:48,159
But here, it didn't.

140
00:08:50,389 --> 00:08:54,399
The Communist Party kept its
monopoly on national political power.

141
00:08:55,689 --> 00:08:58,359
Economic reform did not
turn into political reform.

142
00:08:59,234 --> 00:09:01,804
Which tells us something
worth being careful about.

143
00:09:02,794 --> 00:09:07,004
Economic development can create the
conditions that make democracy possible.

144
00:09:07,908 --> 00:09:09,638
It does not make it inevitable.

145
00:09:12,648 --> 00:09:13,998
Institutions matter.

146
00:09:15,068 --> 00:09:16,518
Civil society matters.

147
00:09:17,528 --> 00:09:21,028
It matters whether opposition
groups can organise, compete, and

148
00:09:21,108 --> 00:09:24,618
eventually take power without destroying
the system they are replacing.

149
00:09:25,569 --> 00:09:28,669
And most of all, the people
currently holding power need

150
00:09:28,749 --> 00:09:31,939
some reason to believe that
losing will not destroy them.

151
00:09:32,577 --> 00:09:35,747
That is also why this is so hard
to predict from the outside.

152
00:09:37,277 --> 00:09:41,067
Sometimes a regime is overthrown.
Sometimes it collapses.

153
00:09:42,117 --> 00:09:43,867
Sometimes it loses a war.

154
00:09:44,605 --> 00:09:46,525
And sometimes the people
running the dictatorship

155
00:09:46,605 --> 00:09:49,315
quietly take parts of it apart themselves.

156
00:09:49,865 --> 00:09:52,195
Not because they became saints.

157
00:09:52,685 --> 00:09:54,915
Because the arithmetic changed.

158
00:09:55,713 --> 00:09:57,613
Which makes Taiwan's story more awkward

159
00:09:57,693 --> 00:10:00,223
than good democracy
beats bad dictatorship.

160
00:10:01,373 --> 00:10:03,103
The dictatorship came first.

161
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:07,530
The economic transformation
happened under that dictatorship.

162
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:13,590
The civil society that eventually demanded
political freedom grew up inside it.

163
00:10:14,234 --> 00:10:15,554
And some of the people who took the

164
00:10:15,634 --> 00:10:18,964
authoritarian system
apart were products of it.

165
00:10:19,599 --> 00:10:21,029
That is the paradox.

166
00:10:21,919 --> 00:10:26,149
A dictatorship can create the conditions
that make dictatorship hard to keep.

167
00:10:26,929 --> 00:10:29,299
And there is a deeper one underneath it.

168
00:10:29,929 --> 00:10:33,299
A dictator does not have to
love democracy to allow it.

169
00:10:33,889 --> 00:10:36,719
He only has to believe
that he can survive losing.

170
00:10:37,568 --> 00:10:39,848
We usually imagine
democracy beginning when

171
00:10:39,928 --> 00:10:44,638
ordinary people demand the right
to choose. That is half of it.

172
00:10:45,346 --> 00:10:47,216
The other half is much quieter.

173
00:10:48,266 --> 00:10:50,666
For any of it to hold, the
people who already have power

174
00:10:50,746 --> 00:10:53,516
have to accept something
that sounds almost trivial.

175
00:10:54,319 --> 00:10:55,519
They might lose.

176
00:10:58,620 --> 00:11:02,870
And when they do, nothing terrible
has to happen. They go home.

177
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:05,210
They form an opposition party.

178
00:11:05,860 --> 00:11:07,550
They criticise the new government.

179
00:11:08,493 --> 00:11:12,143
They campaign again. And
sometimes they win again.

180
00:11:12,953 --> 00:11:16,503
The peaceful transfer of power
works because losing is survivable.

181
00:11:17,169 --> 00:11:19,459
That is the trick. That
is the whole trick.

182
00:11:22,259 --> 00:11:25,559
So a regime that once jailed
its political opponents ended

183
00:11:25,639 --> 00:11:28,809
up building a system in which
those opponents could win.

184
00:11:29,879 --> 00:11:31,849
And the old rulers could accept it.

185
00:11:32,533 --> 00:11:35,543
No revolution. No civil war. A ballot box.

186
00:11:38,005 --> 00:11:40,235
Not a dictatorship that was defeated.

187
00:11:41,285 --> 00:11:43,285
A dictatorship that
turned into a place where

188
00:11:43,365 --> 00:11:45,815
losing was safer than refusing to change.
